use \\(P\\) contains at least one pair of points with a common \\(x\\), \\(y\\) or sum (e.g. \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) share \\(x=1\\)), that pair would have to be collinear on a non‑sunny line different from \\(L\\), contradicting the requirement that exactly one line be non‑sunny.  \nTherefore \\(L\\) must contain **at least two** points of \\(P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  All possible non‑sunny lines containing ≥2 points\n\nFrom the description of \\(P\\) we obtain the following six lines of forbidden slope that contain at least two points of \\(P\\):\n\n| type | equation | points of \\(P\\) on it |\n|------|-----------|-----------------------|\n| vertical | \\(x=1\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) |\n| vertical | \\(x=2\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) |\n| horizontal | \\(y=1\\) | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) |\n| horizontal | \\(y=2\\) | \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) |\n| slope \\(-1\\) | \\(x+y=3\\) | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) |\n| slope \\(-1\\) | \\(x+y=4\\) | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) |\n\nSince there is exactly one non‑sunny line, it must be one of the six lines above.  \nFor each candidate we examine the set of points of \\(P\\) **not** lying on it.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Elimination of each candidate\n\nLet \\(R\\) denote the remaining points after removing those on the chosen non‑sunny line.  \nIf \\(R\\) contains a pair of points that share an \\(x\\), a \\(y\\), or a sum, those two points would have to be collinear on a non‑sunny line distinct from the chosen one, contradicting the hypothesis of having only one non‑sunny line.  \n\n| Candidate for \\(L\\) | Points on \\(L\\) | Remaining set \\(R\\) | Forbidden pair in \\(R\\) |\n|----------------------|------------------|---------------------|------------------------|\n| \\(x=1\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | \\((2,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) share \\(y=1\\) |\n| \\(x=2\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) | \\((1,1),(1