 exists**.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions of steps\u202f2 and\u202f3 show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\); the impossibility argument of step\u202f4 shows that no larger \\(k\\) can occur. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=0\\;(x\\text{-axis}),\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\;x=0\\;(y\\text{-axis}),\\qquad \n\\ell_3:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a **non\u2011sunny** line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\ny=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Configurations with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  \nThus all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered and the number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Configurations with \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nand add one sunny line that passes through the only point of \\(S_n\\) not lying on any of these vertical lines, namely \\((n,1)\\).  \nFor example  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{sunny}}:\\;y-1=2\\,(x-n)\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) are covered (the points with \\(a=n\\) are covered by \\(L_{\\text{sunny}}\\); the others by the vertical lines).  \nExactly one line is sunny, so \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing at least two sunny lines.  \nLet the two sunny lines be \\(U_1,U_2\\); the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\nDenote the two leftmost columns of the triangle \\(S_n\\) by  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad \nC_2=\\{(2,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth columns together contain  \n\n\\[\n|C_1\\cup C_2|=n+(n-1)=2n-1\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nEvery line (sunny or non\u2011sunny) meets the set \\(C_1\\cup C_2\\) in **at most two** points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA vertical line meets at most one of the two columns; a horizontal line meets each column in at most one point; a line of slope \\(-1\\) also meets each column in at most one point.  \nA sunny line is by definition not vertical, not horizontal, and not of slope \\(-1\\); consequently it can intersect each column in at most one point, i.e. at most two points in total. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nEach sunny line meets \\(C_1\\cup C_2\\) in **at most one** point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line is not vertical, hence it cannot contain two points having the same \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \nSince the two columns have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates (1 and 2), a sunny line can intersect at most one of them, and therefore at most one point of the union. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 the two sunny lines together contribute at most \\(2\\) incidences with \\(C_1\\cup C_2\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 each of the remaining \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines contributes at most \\(2\\) incidences.  \nHence the total number of incidences of the whole family \\(\\mathcal F\\) with \\(C_1\\cup C_2\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n2\\;+\\;(n-2)\\cdot 2 = 2n-2 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBut by (1) we need at least \\(2n-1\\) incidences to cover every point of \\(C_1\\cup C_2\\).  \nInequality (2) contradicts this requirement.  \nTherefore a covering with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nIf a covering uses at least three sunny lines, then it uses **at most** \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. it leaves at least three whole columns of the triangle uncovered by non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line can be chosen to be vertical, thereby covering an entire column.  \nWith at most \\(n-3\\) such lines we can cover at most \\(n-3\\) columns, leaving at least three columns uncovered. \u220e\n\nConsider the three leftmost uncovered columns; they contain  \n\n\\[\nn+(n-1)+(n-2)=3n-3\n\\]\n\npoints.  \nEach sunny line meets a given column in at most one point (Lemma\u202f2).  \nThus three sunny lines can meet the three columns in at most \\(3\\) points each, i.e. at most \\(9\\) points altogether, which is far fewer than \\(3n-3\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nConsequently a covering with three or more sunny lines is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombining the two lemmas we have shown that **no covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n* a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists (vertical lines);\n* a configuration with \\(k=1\\) sunny line exists (vertical lines for the first \\(n-1\\) columns and one sunny line through \\((n,1)\\));\n* no configuration exists with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines (proved above).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\; } .\n\\]"

