(k=n\\).\n\n3. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy (4).  \n   Write \\(t=n-k\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines we shall use).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   Take the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate belongs to\n   \\(\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\).  \n   The set of uncovered points is therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   U:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge t+1\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed \\(a\\ge t+1\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n   \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |U|=\\sum_{a=t+1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   We shall cover the set \\(U\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, each line\n   containing precisely two points of \\(U\\) except possibly one line that\n   contains a single point when \\(k\\) is odd.  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i:=\\bigl(t+i,\\;i\\bigr),\\qquad\n   Q_i:=\\bigl(t+i,\\;k+1-i\\bigr).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(t+i\\ge t+1\\) and \\(i\\le k\\le n\\), both points belong to \\(U\\);\n   moreover \\(i\\le k+1-i\\) for \\(i\\le\\frac{k+1}{2}\\), so the two points are\n   distinct unless \\(i=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) (the middle index when \\(k\\) is odd).\n\n   For each \\(i\\) let \\(L_i\\) be the line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k+1-i-i}{t+i-t-i}= \\frac{k+1-2i}{\\,k-2i+1\\,}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The numerator and denominator are never zero simultaneously; the slope\n   can never be \\(0\\) (that would require \\(k+1-2i=0\\) and \\(k-2i+1\\neq0\\)),\n   cannot be \\(\\infty\\) (the denominator never vanishes), and it is never\n   \\(-1\\) because the equality \\(\\frac{k+1-2i}{k-2i+1}=-1\\) would give\n   \\(k+1-2i=-(k-2i+1)\\), i.e. \\(k+1-2i=-k+2i-1\\), whence \\(2k=0\\), impossible\n   for \\(k\\ge1\\).  Hence e