\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines.**\n\n5. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let the required number of sunny lines be \\(k\\) (with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).\n   *If \\(k=0\\)*, take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all\n   are non\u2011sunny and they clearly cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(k\\ge1\\)*, put \\(t=n-k\\) and use the \\(t\\) vertical lines\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,t).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   These \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose first\n   coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).  \n   The still uncovered points are\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge t+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   Writing \\(a'=a-t\\) we have \\(a',b\\ge1\\) and\n   \\(a'+b\\le n-t+1=k+1\\); thus \\(U\\) is exactly the set \\(S_k\\)\n   (translated by the vector \\((t,0)\\)).  \n   By step\u202f4, \\(S_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n   Translating those lines by \\((t,0)\\) yields \\(k\\) sunny lines covering\n   \\(U\\).\n\n   Consequently the family consisting of the \\(t\\) vertical lines (5)\n   together with the translated sunny lines covers **all** points of\n   \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Because the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying the\n   bound (3), the bound is sharp.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The inequality (3) gives the maximal possible number of sunny lines,\n   and the construction of \u00a75 shows that every integer not exceeding that\n   bound is attainable.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n   exactly the one stated in the verdict. \u220e\n\n--- \n\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\quad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\quad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
