tly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(S_n\\), and has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   This construction works for every \\(k\\) described in the statement, proving sufficiency.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The inequality (2) gives the absolute upper bound for the number of sunny lines, while the parity argument shows that only odd values (and \\(0\\)) can be attained. The explicit construction of §5 shows that every admissible value is indeed attainable. Hence the set of all possible numbers \\(k\\) is exactly the one announced in the verdict. ∎\n\n--- \n\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\{0\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}\\mid k\\text{ odd and }k\\le \n\\begin{cases}\nn      &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\nn-1    &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}\n\\end{cases}\\}\\;}\n\\]"
