e a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (i.e. a sunny line).  \n   Write the line in the form \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) be two distinct integer points of \\(L\\).  \n   Then \\((a'-a,b'-b)=(p,q)\\) with \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) and \\(q=mp\\).  \n   Because \\(m\\neq-1\\) we have \\(p+q\\neq0\\); moreover \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) (otherwise \\(|p|=|q|=1\\) and \\(q=-p\\), which would give \\(m=-1\\)).  \n   Hence the quantity \\(a+b\\) changes by at least \\(2\\) when we move from one integer point of \\(L\\) to the next.  \n   Inside the triangle \\(S_{k}\\) the possible values of \\(a+b\\) are the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\) – exactly \\(k\\) different values.  \n   Consequently a sunny line can contain at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_{k}\\).  \n   The bound is attained, for example, by the line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) when \\(k\\) is odd.\n\n4. **Necessary condition on \\(k\\).**  \n   Suppose \\(S_{k}\\) is covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Using (1),\n\n   \\[\n   |S_{k}|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   The right‑hand side equals \\(\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\) when \\(k\\) is odd and equals \\(\\dfrac{k^{2}}2\\) when \\(k\\) is even.  \n   Hence the inequality can hold only for  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\ \\text{odd}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Sufficiency for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   We prove by induction on the odd integer \\(k=2t+1\\) that \\(S_{k}\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Base \\(k=1\\).*  The single point \\((1,1)\\) is covered by any sunny line through it.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  Assume the statement true for \\(k-2=2t-1\\).  \n   Consider the “outer layer’’  \n\n   \\[\n   H:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_{k}\\mid a+b=k+1\\},\n   \\qquad |H|=k .\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le